<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Cassius Longinus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/42-bce">42 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Cassius Longinus was a Roman senator and general, one of the leaders of the conspiracy that assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 44 BCE. Gathering forces in the East, he defeated the Rhodian fleet in 43 or 42 BCE and sacked the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> for refusing to support him. He took his own life after the first battle of Philippi in 42 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus.jpg" alt="Gaius Cassius Longinus" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pseudo-Corbulo_Musei_Capitolini_MC561.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaius Cassius Longinus was born about 86 BCE into the old Roman family of the gens Cassia, prominent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> since the 6th century BCE. Little is known of his early life, apart from a story that at school he quarrelled with the son of the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>, showing his dislike of despots. He studied philosophy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> under Archelaus of Rhodes and became fluent in Greek. He married Junia Tertia, the daughter of Servilia and so a half-sister of Marcus Brutus, and they had a son born about 60 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 54 BCE Cassius joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> as quaestor on his campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. After Crassus's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Battle of Carrhae</a> in 53 BCE, Cassius led the remaining troops back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and organised its defence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>'s account suggests that Crassus could have avoided the disaster by following Cassius's advice. In 51 BCE Cassius defeated an invading Parthian army under Prince Pacorus and the general Osaces, luring it into an ambush after the Parthians gave up their siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>.</p><p>In the civil war he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and commanded part of his fleet, burning a large part of Caesar's navy off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 48 BCE. After Pompey's defeat at Pharsalus he was overtaken by Caesar and surrendered, and Caesar made him a legate. Cassius became praetor in 44 BCE with the promise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, but was offended when his junior and brother-in-law Brutus received the more senior city praetorship. He was the moving spirit of the plot against Caesar, though Brutus became its leader, and on 15 March 44 BCE he struck Caesar in the chest. According to some accounts he wanted to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> as well, but Brutus dissuaded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cassius went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, amassed twelve legions and defeated Publius Cornelius Dolabella, who took his own life. The Senate confirmed him as governor. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-triumvirate">Second Triumvirate</a> was formed, he joined Brutus at Smyrna, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and then met Brutus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, where their armies proclaimed them imperator. They encamped near Philippi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> against Octavian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a>. In the two battles there Brutus beat Octavian and took his camp, but Antony overran Cassius, who, unaware of Brutus's success, ordered his freedman Pindarus to help him kill himself, on 3 October 42 BCE. Brutus mourned him as the last of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and he was buried at Thassos.</p><p>Cassius followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epicurus">Epicurus</a>, probably converting between 48 and 45 BCE, and scholars debate whether this belief shaped his politics. He appears in Shakespeare's Julius Caesar and in Dante's Inferno, where he is chewed in one of the three mouths of Satan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaius Cassius Longinus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the civil wars after Caesar’s murder, however, Rhodes refused to support his assassins, and in 43 BCE <b>Gaius Cassius</b> defeated its fleet, took the city and seized its ships and treasures.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Caesar crossed the Rubicon in 49 BCE, defeated Pompey at Pharsalus in 48 BCE and was made dictator for life early in 44 BCE, before senators led by Brutus and <b>Cassius</b> murdered him on the Ides of March.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Philippi, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-52" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gaius Cassius Longinus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-52">What was here in 52 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Bear witness for me, Messala, that I am brought into the same necessity as Pompey the Great was before me, of hazarding the liberty of my country upon one battle…&#8221;</p><footer>Plutarch, Life of Brutus &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; on the eve of Philippi, 42 BCE; Plutarch's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
